/// ==================== THE VISIBILITY GATE, PINNED ======================
///
/// Spec §3.3: "enforcement is a gate".  A `private` declaration must not be
/// callable from another module.  This project asserts that, and asserts it
/// through EACH of the three doors independently.
///
/// WHY THREE, AND WHY SEPARATELY.  The first attempt at this gate
/// (2026-08-04, docs/name-resolution.md §8.1e) shut two doors and looked like
/// it worked: the compiler and stdlib self-built clean and a private call
/// still compiled through the third.  Shutting any two is indistinguishable
/// from shutting all three unless each is named and observed, so each door
/// here has its OWN private callee and `test.json` requires all three names
/// in the output.  One shared callee would let a single firing door satisfy
/// the whole assertion.
///
/// WHAT THE BARE DOORS ASSERT.  A bare call binds from the name layer's
/// answer for the leaf, and the name layer binds a bare name only to what
/// the writer may see: this module's own declarations, an import it vetted
/// (`visibility_import_gate` pins the refusal of one naming a private), or
/// an export set - which a private declaration is never in.  `Vault`'s
/// private `secret` and `stash` are therefore offered to no importer, and
/// the diagnostic doors 2 and 3 assert is "not found": the name does not
/// exist here, because nothing the writer wrote could bring it into scope.
/// A `plain import VisibilityGate::Vault;` binds no names at all.
///
/// The bare-call binder that used to answer these by searching every
/// module's declarations of the leaf, and then judged the one it found for
/// visibility, is deleted; a bare call has no door there any more.
///
/// WHAT WOULD HAVE BROKEN THIS BEFORE 2026-08-05.  The gate needs a "does the
/// use site live in the declaring module?" exemption, and that used to ask
/// `current_module_ns()` -- the ambient scope cursor.  The cursor is parked on
/// whichever module is being processed, not on the module that wrote the
/// call, so the exemption misfired and rejected 204 `fmt_err` calls that never
/// leave `stdlib/fmt/display.cryo`.  The exemption now asks
/// `module_ns_sym_of_file(span.file)` -- where the syntax was written.
///
/// IF THIS PROJECT STARTS PASSING ITS BUILD: a door reopened.  Do not relax
/// `test.json`.
///
/// =======================================================================
namespace VisibilityGate::Main;

import VisibilityGate::Vault;
import VisibilityGate::Vault::{ door };

function main() -> int {
    // DOOR 1 -- written qualified path.  The path names the private
    // declaration outright, and sema's callee gate refuses it (E0353).
    mut n: i32 = VisibilityGate::Vault::latch();

    // DOOR 2 -- bare call, leaf unique program-wide.  Not offered to this
    // module by any import, so the name layer binds nothing and sema reports
    // the name as not found.
    n = n + secret();

    // DOOR 3 -- bare call, leaf declared by Vault AND Annex.  Annex is not
    // imported and Vault's is private: neither is offered here, and the
    // bare name must not be bound to either by a search of the program.
    n = n + stash(1);

    // CONTROLS.  Both must compile silently.  `test.json` excludes the name
    // `Vault::door` from the output: a gate that rejects these is broken in
    // the direction that actually costs -- false positives on correct code.
    n = n + door();
    n = n + VisibilityGate::Vault::door();

    return n as int;
}
